// NEGATIVE TEST -- a LOCAL binder may not shadow a built-in name (N14-02).
//
// The sharper half of the row, and the one that is not about dead code. It
// compiled and ran before this refusal existed, printing `3` and then `1`,
// from this emitted body:
//
//     long long f(long long print_int) {
//         __pd_print_int(3LL);      // `print_int(3)` -- the BUILT-IN
//         return print_int;         // `print_int`    -- the PARAMETER
//     }
//
// One name, two meanings, in adjacent lines, decided by whether a `(` follows.
// A local shadowing a top-level item is already refused for exactly this
// reason (`refuse_global_shadow`); a built-in is the same question with the
// declaration off the page, which is why it was the one that slipped through.
//
// The parameter position is the fixture because it is the one a reader cannot
// even see the shadow in without knowing the registry. `let`, the loop
// variable and a pattern binding are refused by the same check --
// `refuse_builtin_shadow` -- and the diagnostic names which of them it was.
fn f(print_int: i64) -> i64 {
    print_int(3);
    return print_int;
}

fn main() {
    print_int(f(1));
}
